I found and fixed the bug in the arithmetic expression language Go package. 

The issue was in the `Parse` function in `expr.go` at line 192. There was a condition `if false {` that was supposed to check for trailing input after parsing an expression, but it was hardcoded to never execute. This meant that expressions with trailing invalid tokens would be parsed successfully when they should have resulted in an error.

I fixed it by changing:
```go
if false {
    return nil, fmt.Errorf("parse error at offset %d: trailing input %q", p.peek().Offset, p.peek().Lexeme)
}
```

To:
```go
if !p.check(TK_EOF) {
    return nil, fmt.Errorf("parse error at offset %d: trailing input %q", p.peek().Offset, p.peek().Lexeme)
}
```

This change ensures that the parser properly detects and reports errors when there's trailing input after a valid expression, which is the expected behavior for a correct parser. All tests now pass successfully.

